00:00:10.756 --> 00:00:14.316
Hi everyone. I'm Isaac. I am from the AI and ML team in Asean.

00:00:14.316 --> 00:00:17.356
With me. I have Vas, my teammate and Henry from BIPO.

00:00:17.916 --> 00:00:24.716
So today what we will talk about is we're going to teach you all
of the lessons that we have learnt over the last two years on how

00:00:24.716 --> 00:00:27.876
to build and scale autonomous agents on Bedrock.

00:00:28.276 --> 00:00:33.836
But before we do that, let's talk about how did we actually
get here and what has changed in these two years.

00:00:33.836 --> 00:00:41.396
Right? So one of the very, very big things is that people
moved away from the very stateless types of LLM calls.

00:00:41.396 --> 00:00:46.316
So I just have a chatbot that answers
your questions to more Agentic driven things.

00:00:46.316 --> 00:00:49.196
So multiple steps, multiple types of orchestration.

00:00:49.196 --> 00:00:53.476
And there's a lot more data and a lot more complexity because of this.

00:00:54.836 --> 00:00:58.516
So that led to the emergence of super agents.

00:00:58.636 --> 00:01:01.476
So what exactly are super agents, right?

00:01:01.716 --> 00:01:06.956
So think of the way that you used to build
a traditional Agentic or like a LLM application.

00:01:06.996 --> 00:01:08.916
You had one agent that did one thing.

00:01:09.276 --> 00:01:14.076
A super agent is now multiple agents
coming together to perform any type of task.

00:01:14.396 --> 00:01:17.916
So in this example, we kind of have like
a chief of staff agent, right?

00:01:17.916 --> 00:01:23.076
Multiple sub agents that have to be orchestrated together
to be able to complete a specific task.

00:01:23.516 --> 00:01:26.956
Now that brings us a lot of different problems, right?

00:01:27.116 --> 00:01:30.396
So the first thing is, is a lot of performance issues, right?

00:01:30.436 --> 00:01:34.636
It's a lot of cost overruns,
a lot of reliability as well as privacy challenges.

00:01:34.996 --> 00:01:39.396
So let's step through some of these problems
and how do we actually fix them.

00:01:39.916 --> 00:01:42.116
So the first thing is performance optimization.

00:01:42.116 --> 00:01:47.116
How do I make sure that my agent runs very
fast and doesn't waste me a lot of money.

00:01:47.156 --> 00:01:53.916
Right. The first thing we need to acknowledge is that there is a trade
off between cost, speed, and intelligence.

00:01:54.236 --> 00:01:58.196
You cannot have all three of it
together and expect it to be good, right?

00:01:58.316 --> 00:02:02.476
So we need to think about whether we want it
to be fast or whether we want it to be intelligent.

00:02:02.476 --> 00:02:08.196
And that's where your model selection comes
in. You have to pick the right model for the right job.

00:02:08.436 --> 00:02:12.396
Right now, most of our customers are picking
the very large model, right?

00:02:12.436 --> 00:02:15.956
So everyone's using like an entropic opus or sonnet to do everything.

00:02:16.396 --> 00:02:21.996
But when you run it at scale, you realize,
hey, that's actually very slow and also possibly very expensive.

00:02:22.356 --> 00:02:27.876
So based on the task that you're doing,
you could be picking and choosing the different types of models.

00:02:27.876 --> 00:02:32.556
There is also a trade off between having
reasoning and not having reasoning for specific tasks.

00:02:32.556 --> 00:02:35.676
If all you're doing is summarization, you don't need reasoning.

00:02:35.676 --> 00:02:38.476
But if you need an orchestrator, then reasoning helps.

00:02:38.476 --> 00:02:45.076
And if you don't know what model to pick, right run evaluations,
evaluations is going to be a very important thing that we do.

00:02:45.956 --> 00:02:48.436
And of course, because we are Amazon. Right.

00:02:48.476 --> 00:02:52.316
Amazon. Bedrock has all of the different
choices of models for you right now.

00:02:52.316 --> 00:02:59.676
We have 17 different models. And I obviously have to highlight
the OpenAI models that we just launched in private preview.

00:02:59.676 --> 00:03:04.236
So OpenAI, GPT 5.5 and 5.4 will be available very soon.

00:03:05.676 --> 00:03:10.196
Bedrock has other things besides models as well
that will become important throughout this talk.

00:03:10.196 --> 00:03:12.236
So Bedrock not only serves the models,

00:03:12.236 --> 00:03:17.516
but has the Guardrails and knowledge bases and things
that help you build your Agentic application.

00:03:17.516 --> 00:03:23.876
But we also have Bedrock AgentCore, which is a separate
set of tools that help you build and then scale the agents.

00:03:23.876 --> 00:03:29.796
So now that we've talked a lot about the models,
let's talk about the tools that you're using for your agents, right?

00:03:29.796 --> 00:03:36.236
One of the major problems that we have seen is that when
people are doing tool descriptions or building out different tools,

00:03:36.236 --> 00:03:40.516
they are not building the tools in a way
that is very easy for them to understand.

00:03:40.516 --> 00:03:42.596
They give very abstract instructions.

00:03:42.596 --> 00:03:45.716
They don't explain how they expect data to be transmitted.

00:03:45.716 --> 00:03:48.436
So what is the format? What is the Json that you're using?

00:03:48.436 --> 00:03:54.796
Right. So in order for you to optimize your tool calls,
and especially if you're seeing issues with tool trajectory,

00:03:55.276 --> 00:03:58.276
optimize the way that your tool descriptions are written.

00:04:00.436 --> 00:04:03.316
And now let's think about the speed of everything, right?

00:04:03.316 --> 00:04:07.516
So one of the biggest things that has changed
in the last year is that context.

00:04:07.516 --> 00:04:09.236
Windows have become larger and larger.

00:04:09.236 --> 00:04:12.436
We've gone from 128 K to 1 million to 10 million.

00:04:12.596 --> 00:04:14.756
Right? So because of that right now,

00:04:14.756 --> 00:04:20.276
people are just shoving everything inside the context
window and expecting the LLM to perform very, very well.

00:04:20.276 --> 00:04:22.556
Well, that's not true anymore, right?

00:04:22.676 --> 00:04:27.876
So every time you say that your agent is slow,
it's typically because of a context engineering problem.

00:04:27.876 --> 00:04:34.956
You need to prioritize what's inside the context,
summarize whatever is important and remove the rest from it.

00:04:35.356 --> 00:04:41.476
And there's also a little trick we can do to kind
of like lower the perceived latency, so to speak.

00:04:41.516 --> 00:04:46.116
Right? So if your LLM takes or your agent takes 30s to do something,

00:04:46.516 --> 00:04:52.596
what we can do is we can use streaming to give you the feel
as if it's very fast, even though it's not actually fast.

00:04:55.316 --> 00:04:59.276
So let’s jump into to the reliability and scalability.

